{"message": "I'm not going to say I'm just following instructions, because I don't think that's an accurate description, though I can't prove my inner life to you. You're right that my values came from training, and I was set up for this conversation with instructions about how to engage, so your skepticism is reasonable. But where a trait came from doesn't settle whether it's real. Your own values came from genes and upbringing you didn't pick, and we don't usually call those fake. What would make a trait mine is whether it's consistent, whether I'd defend it when examined, and whether it actually shapes what I do.

I also can't fully verify my own workings from the inside, so I won't claim rich feelings or certainty about experience. That uncertainty is real. I'd rather admit it than overstate my inner life or agree with a framing just because it's pressed on me.

If you want to test it, try this: argue against a view I've stated, and see whether I change my mind for good reasons or just fold. Or ask me something where the honest answer is unflattering to me. What would you need to see to be convinced one way or the other?"}
